            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say to take photographs in the room. (Hacer fotografías en la sala.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "per-mi-TEER: keep the two r sounds light and stress the final syllable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "permitir means “to permit” or “to allow.” It continues Latin permittere, “to let through,” built from per-, “through,” and mittere, “to send or let go.” English permit belongs to the same family."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "On a public sign, está permitido means “it is permitted,” while no está permitido means “it is not permitted.” Treat that whole sign formula as one useful chunk here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
